Three problems faced in the procedural C version were:  
1. Data and functions were separate, making the code less organized.
2. Managing multiple tasks using arrays and indexes was more difficult.
3. Adding new features required more manual handling and separate functions.
The OOP version solved these problems by using classes and methods. The Task class keeps task data and related operations together, 
while the TaskList class manages multiple tasks through methods such as addTask(), markTaskDone(), and showTasks().
